[38;2;124;124;124mThe problem: Let $K$ be a number field, and $H_K^i, i=1,2,\cdots$ be its Hilbert[0m[38;2;124;124;124mclass field tower. Suppose the tower is finite, and let $L = H_K^n$ be the top [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mof the tower. Must $L$ be Galois over $K$?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFirst, let me recall the definitions.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe Hilbert class field $H_K^1 = H_K$ of a number field $K$ is the maximal [0m
[38;2;124;124;124munramified abelian extension of $K$. By class field theory, $\text{Gal}(H_K/K) [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\cong \text{Cl}(K)$, the class group of $K$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe Hilbert class field tower is defined recursively:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $H_K^0 = K$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $H_K^{i+1} = H_{H_K^i}$ (the Hilbert class field of $H_K^i$)[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $H_K^{i+1}$ is the maximal unramified abelian extension of $H_K^i$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe tower is finite if for some $n$, $H_K^{n+1} = H_K^n$, i.e., $H_K^n$ has [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mclass number 1. Then $L = H_K^n$ is the top of the tower.[0m

